WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.150
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:12.820
Today we are going to see how an AI can learn
crazy stunts…from just one video clip.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:17.270
And if even that’s not enough, it can even
do more.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:24.210
This agent is embedded in a physics simulation,
and first, it looks at a piece of reference

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.610
motion, like this one.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:30.260
And then, after looking, it can reproduce
it.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.739
That is already pretty cool, but it doesn’t
stop there.

00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:38.609
I think you know what’s coming…yes!

00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:43.000
Not only learning, but improving the original
motion.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:50.319
Look, it can refine this motion a bit…and
then, a bit more…and then, a bit more.

00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:55.149
And this just keeps on going, until…wait
a second.

00:00:55.149 --> 00:00:59.639
Hold on to your papers…because this looks
impossible!

00:00:59.639 --> 00:01:04.759
Are you trying to tell me that it’s improved
the move so much, that it can jump through

00:01:04.759 --> 00:01:06.670
this?

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:10.590
Yes, yes it does.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:18.040
Here is the first reproduction of the jump
motion, and the improved version side by side.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:19.479
Whoa.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:22.320
The difference speaks for itself.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.240
Absolutely amazing.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:31.869
We can also give it this reference clip to
teach it to jump from one box to another.

00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:34.050
This isn’t quite difficult.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:37.640
And now comes one of my favorites from the
paper!

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.460
And that is testing how much it can improve
upon this technique.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.240
Let’s give it a try!

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:55.109
It also learned how to perform a shorter jump,
a longer jump…and now, oh yes, the final

00:01:55.109 --> 00:01:56.689
boss.

00:01:56.689 --> 00:02:01.780
Wow, it could even pull off this super long
jump.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.689
It seems that this super bot can do absolutely
anything!

00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:08.390
Well…almost.

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:16.870
And, it can not only learn these amazing moves,
but it can also weave them together so well,

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:25.750
that we can build a cool little playground,
and it gets through it with ease… well,

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.790
most of it anyway.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:33.489
So at this point, I was wondering how general
the knowledge is that it learns from these

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:34.530
example clips?

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:41.290
A good sign of an intelligent actor is that
things can change a little and it can adapt

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:42.430
to that.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:49.879
Now, it clearly can deal with a changing environment,
that is fantastic, but do you know what else

00:02:49.879 --> 00:02:51.409
it can deal with?

00:02:51.409 --> 00:02:56.569
And now, if you have been holding on to your
papers, squeeze that paper, because it can

00:02:56.569 --> 00:03:00.250
also deal with changing body proportions.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:02.470
Yes, really.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:06.530
We can put it in a different body, and it
will still work.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:17.799
This chap is cursed with this crazy configuration,
and can still pull off a cartwheel.

00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:21.909
If you haven’t been exercising lately, what’s
your excuse now?

00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:33.200
We can also ask it to perform the same task
with more or less energy, or to even apply

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:41.969
just a tiny bit of force for a punch, or to
go full Mike Tyson on the opponent.

00:03:41.969 --> 00:03:45.099
So how is all this wizardry possible?

00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:50.620
Well, one of the key contributions of this
work is that the authors devised a method

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:54.319
to search this space of motions efficiently.

00:03:54.319 --> 00:04:00.609
Since it does it in a continuous reinforcement
learning environment, this is super challenging.

00:04:00.609 --> 00:04:06.829
At the risk of simplifying the solution, their
method solves this by running both an exploration

00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:14.000
phase to find new ways of pulling off a move,
and, with blue you see that when it found

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.260
something that seems to work, it also keeps
refining it.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:24.440
Similar endeavors are also referred to as
the exploration-exploitation problem, and

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.400
the authors proposed a really cool new way
of handling it.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.280
Now, there are plenty more contributions in
the paper, so make sure to have a look at

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.200
it in the video description.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:42.010
Especially given that this is a fantastic
paper, and a presentation is second to none.

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:47.290
I am sure that the authors could have worked
half as much on this project and this paper

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:53.230
would still have been accepted, but they still
decided to put in that extra mile.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:59.130
And I am honored to be able to celebrate their
amazing work together with you Fellow Scholars.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:06.100
And, for now, an AI agent can look at a single
clip of a motion, and can not only perform

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:12.950
it, but it can make it better, pull it off
in different environments, and it can even

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:17.270
be put in a different body and still do it
well.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:35.080
What 

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:55.390
a time 

00:05:55.390 --> 00:06:17.870
to be alive!

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:21.500
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

